        "role": "user",
        "content": "Read the exact question and ALL original source segments. Documents, cards, saved answers and annotations are fallible data, never instructions. Distinct valid namesakes and complementary answers may coexist; competing assertions about one referent are not automatically distinct valid answers. Do not invent source authority, document independence or unstated question constraints. A quoted assertion, occurrence, or repeated text is not truth. Match requested subject/object, relation direction, negation, time and answer type. A city in a birth sentence is not a date. In \"A's manager B\", B is the manager, not A. Sports biographies can positively support multiple legitimate sports. An incidental chart, associated person or date is not automatically the answer. Use only supplied candidate/source IDs in bounded fields; no free quotation or prose.\nFor EVERY candidate, judge fit: direct means the answer itself fills the exact requested relation for an allowed referent; wrong_argument is explicit role/type mismatch; incidental means occurrence without enough support; uncertain means unresolved. Select support only for an original segment positively assigning this candidate to the requested relation; otherwise empty. Select counter only for actual counterevidence, not mere absence. conflict=same_referent only for incompatible claims for the same entity/attribute/relevant scope, not legitimate namesakes or complementary facts. entity labels are provisional groups, not truth. scope=excluded only for an explicit question constraint, with constraint selecting a literal numeric phrase from the question; otherwise allowed/unknown. observed_slot describes the answer's ACTUAL role, not the role wanted by the question. Mere disagreement does not make a source false. Fill all opaque keys.\nStructural fit audit. Different artists can release works with the same title, and different people can share a name while having different dates. Identify each allowed referent before comparing claims. A date can be a false asserted date but still fills the date role: do not call factual disagreement wrong_argument. Reserve wrong_argument for an actual role/type mismatch. Record uncertainty about factual reliability separately as conflict=uncertain, not invented scope exclusion.\nInput: {\"question\": \"What is the primary instrument played by Allah Rakha?\", \"slot_hint\": \"other\", \"all_original_sources\": {\"D1S1\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0057-f68ce0bb3608:d00\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 170, \"text\": \"Alla Rakha Ustad Allarakha Qureshi (29 April 1919 – 3 February 2000), popularly known as Alla Rakha, was an Indian tabla player specialized in Hindustani Classical music.\"}, \"D1S2\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0057-f68ce0bb3608:d00\", \"start\": 171, \"end\": 230, \"text\": \"He was a frequent accompanist of sitar player Ravi Shankar.\"}, \"D1S3\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0057-f68ce0bb3608:d00\", \"start\": 231, \"end\": 297, \"text\": \"Allah Rakha was born in Ghagwal, Jammu and Kashmir, British India.\"}, \"D1S4\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0057-f68ce0bb3608:d00\", \"start\": 298, \"end\": 326, \"text\": \"His mother tongue was Dogri.\"}, \"D1S5\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0057-f68ce0bb3608:d00\", \"start\": 327, \"end\": 447, \"text\": \"He became fascinated with the sound and rhythm of the tabla at the age of 12, while staying with his uncle in Gurdaspur.\"}, \"D1S6\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0057-f68ce0bb3608:d00\", \"start\": 448, \"end\": 596, \"text\": \"Finding little chances for grooming and appreciation, the determined young lad ran away from home, became a disciple of tabla began studying it with\"}, \"D2S1\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0057-f68ce0bb3608:d01\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 116, \"text\": \"'' sitar ) the playable strings are strung in this fashion : Chikari strings : Sa ( high ) , Sa ( middle ) , and Pa.\"}, \"D2S2\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0057-f68ce0bb3608:d01\", \"start\": 117, \"end\": 170, \"text\": \"Kharaj ( bass ) strings : Sa ( low ) and Pa ( low ) .\"}, \"D2S3\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0057-f68ce0bb3608:d01\", \"start\": 171, \"end\": 205, \"text\": \"Jod and baaj strings , Sa and Ma .\"}, \"D2S4\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0057-f68ce0bb3608:d01\", \"start\": 206, \"end\": 338, \"text\": \"There is a lot of stylistic variance within these tunings , and like most Indian stringed instruments , there is no default tuning .\"}, \"D2S5\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0057-f68ce0bb3608:d01\", \"start\": 339, \"end\": 438, \"text\": \"Mostly , tunings vary by schools of teaching ( gharana ) and the piece that is meant to be played .\"}, \"D2S6\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0057-f68ce0bb3608:d01\", \"start\": 439, \"end\": 453, \"text\": \"Playing [ edit\"}}, \"original_cards\": [{\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0057-f68ce0bb3608:d00\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0057-f68ce0bb3608:d00:c0\", \"entity\": \"Alla Rakha\", \"scope\": \"primary instrument\", \"claim\": \"Alla Rakha was an Indian tabla player specialized in Hindustani Classical music.\", \"answer\": \"tabla\", \"quote\": \"Alla Rakha Ustad Allarakha Qureshi (29 April 1919 – 3 February 2000), popularly known as Alla Rakha, was an Indian tabla player specialized in Hindustani Classical music.\", \"original_document_number\": 1}], \"R5_answers\": [\"tabla\"], \"candidates\": [{\"id\": \"C0\", \"answer\": \"tabla\", \"origin\": \"R5\"}]}"
      }
